# `M154g` step 2d / `C77` — `declaration:action`, and the half of its contract that 51 existing
# `action` declarations in this repository do not observe.
#
# The manifest's sentence is two claims joined by a "so":
#
#   "the language's only unit of reuse; gets its own response scope, so a call never publishes its
#    response to the caller (`FU-12`)"
#
# The first clause — reuse — is what every existing use demonstrates: declare `create order(...)`,
# call it from four tests, read what it `give`s back. The second is a claim about the caller's
# state **after** the call returns, and nothing here has ever looked. The ordinary shape of a test
# is that a call is followed by an assertion on the value it gave, not by an assertion on `body`,
# so an action that leaked its own response into the caller's scope would be invisible in all 51.
#
# The test below is deliberately written in the one order that can tell the two apart: the caller
# makes a request, the action makes a *later* one, and then the caller asserts on `body` again. If
# a call published its response, the last line reads the action's `c77inner` and goes red. `give`
# being the only channel out is asserted in the same test, one line above, so the file states both
# directions of `FU-12` rather than only the negative.
#
# Third reading, and the reason the negative means anything: the grader also requires the server to
# have seen `c77inner` exactly once. Without it, an `action` that was never invoked at all — or one
# whose body was skipped — passes the last line trivially, because `body` would still be the
# caller's for the most boring possible reason.

before file
  api POST /lifecycle/reset
  expect status equals 200

action c77 marks inside(label)
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "{label}" }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.label as marked
  give marked

@constructs @declarations
test "an action's response never becomes the caller's — `give` is the only channel out"
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c77outer" }
  expect status equals 200
  expect body.label equals "c77outer"

  # The positive half: the value crosses, and it crosses by being `give`n.
  let inner = c77 marks inside("c77inner")
  expect {inner} equals "c77inner"

  # `FU-12`. The action issued a strictly later request whose response echoed `c77inner`; if a call
  # published its response to its caller, this reads `c77inner` and the test is red.
  expect body.label equals "c77outer"
